{"name":"Thunderbird","hex":"#923830","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Thunderbird","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of thunderbird.","A locomotive stored at a strategic point on the network so as to be available to quickly rescue a failed train."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Mozilla Thunderbird.","Ford Thunderbird."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Thunderbird"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thunderbird (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"The thunderbird is a mythological birdlike spin North American Indigenous peoples' history and culture. It is considered a supernatural being of power and strength."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Thunderbird_on_Totem_Pole.jpg/330px-Thunderbird_on_Totem_Pole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunderbird_(mythology)"},{"title":"Dromornithidae","blocks":[{"text":"Dromornithidae, known as mihirungs and informally as thunder birds or demon ducks, is an extinct family of large, flightless birds native to Australia from at least the late Oligocene to the Late Pleistocene. They were long classified in Struthioniformes, but are now usually classified as a type of gigantic fowl (Galloanserae). Dromornithids were part of the Australian megafauna. One species, Dromornis stirtoni, was 3 m tall, making them among the largest birds ever. Only a single species, Genyornis newtoni survived into the Late Pleistocene. They are thought to have been herbivorous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Dromornis_stirtoni_01.jpg/330px-Dromornis_stirtoni_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dromornithidae"},{"title":"Thunderbird (wine)","blocks":[{"text":"Thunderbird was a discount, flavored fortified wine marketed by E. & J. Gallo Winery in the United States. 